Both UNION and UNION ALL are set operators 
used to stack the results of two or more SELECT queries vertically.
The fundamental difference is how they handle identical rows: UNION ALL simply appends everything together, 
keeping duplicates, whereas UNION actively removes all duplicate rows before returning the final result.

Technical Breakdown :-
UNION ALL (The Append): The engine takes the output of Query A and sticks it directly on top of the output of Query B.
It's a fast, blind concatenation. It requires almost zero processing overhead.

UNION (The Deduplicator): The engine takes Query A and Query B, appends them, and then must perform an implicit DISTINCT operation across the entire combined dataset.
To do this, the engine usually has to sort all the rows or build a massive hash table in memory to identify and drop the duplicates.
This adds significant computational cost and memory overhead.

Business Context :-

The choice depends on the data context. 
If you are combining a mailing list of 'recent buyers' and 'newsletter subscribers' to send a promo code,
you must use UNION so a customer in both lists doesn't receive two emails.
But if you are stacking '2022 Sales' and '2023 Sales', those are mutually exclusive by definition.
Using UNION ALL gets you the exact same result but finishes in a fraction of the time

Design Decision :-

In modern data engineering across distributed systems like Snowflake, BigQuery, or Apache Spark, using a standard UNION when you don't absolutely need it is a cardinal sin.
Deduplicating massive datasets forces the cluster into an expensive network 'shuffle', moving terabytes of data between worker nodes just to check for duplicates.
My default standard is to always write UNION ALL.
I only use UNION if I have proven that duplicates exist and that the business logic explicitly requires them to be destroyed.
